l  Forces team decided as a whole that they  did not want their names use the only  names in the book are those that the  individual soldiers agreed to have used  and in general many of the soldiers are  either retired nearing retirement or at  least finished with their front-line  careers so using their name would not  jeopardize their families or themselves  as they would not be conducting  frontline missions anymore  also have things important just so you  know the a few of the details about  exactly how the Special Forces operate  we are not in the book and this was by  agreement I was given some basic ground  rules by the command and it's important  for them to have certain tactics  preserve them for future use and  certainly as a reporter I felt this was  a fair compromise since I got ninety  nine percent of the story and the for  the sake of preserving the utility of  their tactics I felt this was a fair  trade-off so with that I'm going to open  it up for any comments or questions any  stories that you folks want to tell  there anyone who would like to say  anything  learner yeah battle in the Western  Desert was that on the training talked  about between Nellis and the Special  Forces that kind of a base on the OE f  lessons learned as far as closed course  air support now during a rapid lessons  learned absolutely yes that that is  exactly right and while of course  Afghanistan Operation Enduring Freedom  was extremely successful in terms of a  very rapid takedown of the Taliban the  Afghan government and capturing quite a  number of al-qaeda cohorts there were  friendly casualties there were  difficulties and again it is absolutely  understandable that you have fast movers  farmers up above very high up it's very  difficult to distinguish between small  targets thousands and thousands of feet  below and also there have been some  difficulties at times using equipment  laser target designators and this  rigorous system that they worked out in  the western 