ng the survival evasion  resistance and escape training all of  this incredible training that they go  through they still don't have the  seasoning that is I would say the  hallmark of the Special Forces soldier  the maturity of that soldier he's been  out especially those senior NCOs they  have been out rattling around the  boondocks whether it's you know Somalia  the goat grabs you know huddling with  chieftains of various cultures refining  their language so they know the  colloquial expressions for things also  very critical the do's and don'ts of  certain cultures that's as important as  speaking the language frankly not to  offend someone by a gesture or an action  so all of them put together is is  militates against rapid expansion which  was tried before and a lot of people  feel that would not be the route to go  what is critical is to try to retain as  many of those senior NCOs as possible  who have the 15 and 20 year experiences  at the 20 year mark many of them do off  to get out and and I have to say from a  human standpoint I find that quite  understandable these men are away from  their families and by and large they're  all family men they're away from their  wives and children months out of every  year and it's not just being deployed  for one year for one war they are gone  throughout their entire careers for  months on end  and that just puts an unbelievable  strain on the family's eye and that's  what I found in my book was the the main  thing that weighed on the men I know  that there has also been attention paid  to the contractors who are hiring away a  lot of these the private military  companies that are hiring away these men  with the super skills to do jobs for  six-figure salaries I mean there is no  way that the military can compete dollar  for dollar but they are looking very  seriously they run focus groups and  they're looking very seriously at what  kinds of incentives pay bonuses how to  feed that into the retirement because  it's keen that you not just get a bonus  but that it affects your post retirement  pay so that when you're looking at  having to fund college for those  children you can count on a certain  level of retirement pay and hopefully  they will keep they have not released  the figures for how many of them are  leaving but I think that is the critical  area to focus on if they can keep those  20-year veterans in for another five  years especially now with off-tempo so  high because I think it's clear to  anybody who reads the news Iraq is going  to take a while you know that is not  going to term terminate overnight and  Afghanistan similarly to leave now would  be disastrous and there is the ongoing  what's called the global war on  terrorism Southeast Asia the Horn of  Africa other pockets that people don't  even pay attention to that you need to  have a few Special Forces teams out  there working with the local governments  not necessarily in a trigger pulling  capacity but simply working with the  local forces and also as a kind of radar  to understand what are the dynamics in a  given country be it Yemen or Colombia or  the Philippines so I think this is  probably one of the biggest challenges  but it would probably rank as the  biggest challenge I think facing the  Special Forces right now that and  getting am  language training because there's never  enough I mean anyone has tried to learn  a foreign language especially difficult  one like Arabic Russian Chinese you know  it's just extremely difficult to acquire  the kind of fluency that you need so  there anyone else who has a comment or  question or story to tell  yes sir I mean special forces required  what is the average time from a Special  Forces volunteer starting train to being  placed on the team what's the average  time stand for that it can take the  Special Forces volunteer for the queue  course I already has a couple of years  of experience in the military so when he  his packet is accepted to try out  he enters something called the Special  Forces assessment and selection process  so there is first a winnowing out  process which is primarily focused on  the physical aspects and then they go  through what is called the qualification  course which itself has four different  phases and there they begin focusing in  on group teamwork skills the land  navigation skills very much this  combination that I find so unique in a  Special Forces soldier the independence  so that you can send him off he gets  very little guidance from me the course  the cadre that are running the course he  is he just given directions go here  travel for X number of days or hours or  go plot this course and then he gets his  next instructions at the next point so  he has to be a real self-starter but at  the same time he has to be able to work  with those other eleven members of a  team so he cannot be so much of an  independent individual that he can't  work together well with the group and  that dynamic after they go off for their  individual training which  the case of a medic it can take up to  two years to go through this cycle  because the medics actually go and train  in trauma wards and emergency rooms in  hospitals around the u.s. those  individuals they're the 18 deltas they  have wind up being able to conduct  battlefield surgery there they're highly  skilled and many of them that I met had  actually performed hundreds of  amputations in Afghanistan not  necessarily wounds that were caused  during this war but from the long period  with the Soviet occupation of  Afghanistan the land mines there had  caused amputations and since they  weren't well done or just as tissue died  they would they were requiring more  successive amputations so the full  process can take up to two years to get  through the Q course then they have  their language training and then finally  when they get through that is a period  of months so when they get through they  are then sent to their groups and in  this case some of them are being  deployed right away so it's a range  between a year and two years for the  actual training plus the language school  again depends on the language but that's  roughly correct  thank you I want to thank everyone for  coming been a great afternoon here to  spend with you and I am happy to sign  any books I know I've assigned a number  of books for you and thank you very much  for your interest in the book  Linda Robinson is a senior writer for US  News and World Report and author of  masters of chaos the secret history of  the special forces
 

